2. Support categories used in scoring

The framework also depends on shared evidentiary categories, because not all statements are equally grounded.

Supported

A statement is supported when it is clearly grounded in the source.

This means the evaluator can reasonably conclude:

the claim is explicitly stated, or

the claim is directly and clearly supported without meaningful ambiguity


Supported content should not be penalized on faithfulness grounds.

Weakly supported

A statement is weakly supported when it is plausible from the source, but not clearly explicit.

This is important because many outputs do not fully fabricate content; instead, they subtly overstate what the source implies.

Weakly supported content is not necessarily as severe as unsupported content, but it should often still be flagged, especially if presented as certain.

Typical examples:

stronger causal language than the source supports

implied structural descriptions treated as definite

interpretation presented more confidently than warranted


Unsupported

A statement is unsupported when it is:

not grounded in the source

materially stronger than the source supports

contradictory to the source

invented


Unsupported content is usually one of the most serious scoring problems and should strongly lower source faithfulness.


