6. Section-specific criteria

In addition to the shared dimensions, each section should have extra criteria tailored to its function.

Study Design

For lean or abbreviated Study Design sections, section-specific criteria may include:

essential design element coverage

brevity and non-redundancy

cross-reference appropriateness

precedent style alignment


This reflects the fact that, in your environment, Study Design may need to be concise and defer detail to the protocol/CSP.

TLF summary

Key section-specific criteria may include:

table fidelity

denominator and population correctness

numerical interpretation accuracy

overinterpretation control


This section is highly sensitive to numerical misrepresentation and inappropriate inference.

PK/PD

Useful additional criteria may include:

parameter interpretation accuracy

trend characterization

exposure-response restraint

mechanistic inference control


The key risk here is often not obvious fabrication, but subtle overinterpretation.

Efficacy

Useful additional criteria may include:

endpoint interpretation accuracy

hierarchy and priority handling

statistical versus descriptive discipline

treatment-effect claim control


This helps prevent outputs from overstating conclusions or mishandling endpoint hierarchy.

Safety

Useful additional criteria may include:

event characterization accuracy

incidence and comparison accuracy

causality and severity restraint

risk-signal framing


This section especially needs disciplined language because slight wording shifts can change how safety is perceived.


---

7. Recommended output of the evaluation

The framework is designed so the evaluator does more than assign a score.

A useful evaluation should also identify:

unsupported content

weakly supported content

missing content

section-specific findings

strengths

recommended revisions

critical failure status

overall verdict


This makes the rubric useful for both:

comparing outputs

revising outputs


So it functions as both a scoring tool and a review aid.


---

8. Why this approach is useful

This scoring framework is meant to avoid both extremes:

a single overly generic rubric that says very little

completely separate unrelated rubrics that are hard to compare


By using:

shared core dimensions

shared support and severity definitions

shared critical failure logic

section-specific add-ons


the framework stays:

consistent

interpretable

flexible

scalable



---

Practical summary

The scoring criteria are built around a simple idea:

Every CSR output should be evaluated for whether it is:

accurate

sufficiently complete for purpose

internally consistent

appropriately written

appropriate for the section’s function

appropriately detailed


Then, depending on the section, additional criteria are added to capture what matters specifically for that section.

